WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
Hey there, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners episode 80.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.440
It's June 22nd, 2026.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.060
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.320
Let's get into it.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:17.020
Something wild just happened with the way people might use AI on their computers from now

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:17.320
on.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.220
Open AI added a feature called Record Replay inside their Codex app.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:29.080
You do a task once on your Mac, the system watches every move, and then it can repeat that

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.320
exact task for you whenever you ask.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:36.400
Think of it like teaching someone to fold laundry by letting them watch you do it one time,

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.240
after which they handle the whole pile without any further directions.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:47.900
The AI captures your clicks, drags, and keystrokes on screen, then turns those steps into a reusable

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.520
skill it stores away.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:55.980
Later you trigger the skill with one short command, and the AI replays the sequence on its

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:56.200
own.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:02.060
Picture this like a player piano that learns a song after you press the keys once, then

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.660
performs it perfectly every time you press play.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.320
This shifts AI from just answering questions to actually carrying out repeatable work you

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.060
set up yourself.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.820
Students could show the AI how they organize notes or pull data for weekly projects.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.200
Then reuse the process without repeating the clicks.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.460
Creatives might demonstrate resizing and tagging batches of images, freeing up time for

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.720
the parts of making art they enjoy most.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.020
For you this could mean fewer boring clicks when handling schoolwork, side projects, or content

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:36.560
creation.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.380
Instead of typing the same instructions over and over, you demonstrate the workflow once

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:43.920
and let the AI take over.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:50.680
The feature is starting on Mac OS first, though it is not yet available in the EU, UK, or

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:51.240
Switzerland.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:57.380
You can prepare for it right now by going to the open AI website, signing up for the free

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:04.080
tier of chat GPT and practicing clear, step-by-step descriptions of tasks you do often.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.460
That practice will make your demonstrations even stronger once record replay reaches more

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:09.820
people.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.940
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive, where we look under the hood.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.380
You know how when you show a friend how to make your favorite sandwich, you just do it once

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.360
in front of them and they remember the steps.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:28.380
Now imagine the friend is an AI that turns your exact movements into a saved recipe, it can

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.760
follow perfectly every single time without asking questions.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:38.100
First, the AI watches every click, drag, and keystroke you make while doing the task on your

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:38.560
computer.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.760
Next, it breaks those actions into small repeatable chunks it can store as a skill.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.340
Then it saves that skill so you can trigger it later with one simple command instead of

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:51.480
doing everything again.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.840
Finally, when you ask it to run the skill, the AI replays the exact sequence on its own.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.680
Just like following the sandwich recipe without you standing there.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.980
Think of each stored chunk like a single Lego brick that snaps together with the others

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.120
to rebuild the full structure any time.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:15.280
Another way to see it is like recording a short video of your hands on a piano and then

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.020
letting a robot hand copy those exact finger movements later.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.780
And that's basically what record replay is doing when it turns your one time demonstration

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.640
into an automatic workflow.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:33.500
So next time someone says AI agents learning from demonstration, you can tell them it is basically

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.460
recording your sandwich making once so the robot can make it forever.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.100
Not so scary, right?

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:41.820
Let's look at some cool stuff you can try today.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:49.120
One article shares 28 practical ways to get better answers from chat GPT by changing how

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.140
you ask questions.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:56.240
Instead of vague requests, you give the AI more structure, examples, or roles to play.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.860
This matters because better prompts mean the AI actually helps with homework, story ideas,

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:04.940
or planning, instead of giving generic replies.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:10.080
Anyone who uses chat GTT for school or creative project should try it.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:17.340
Go to chatgtt.com, pick a task like help me plan a weekend project, and test one tip by adding

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.480
the phrase, explain it like I'm 14 at the end of your prompt.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.900
Watch how the answer becomes clearer and more useful once you give the model that extra guidance.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:33.280
The article explains that providing roles, examples, or added structure helps avoid generic

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.020
replies on tasks such as homework help.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:42.200
Another fun tool lets you search how much an AI model knows about you or any topic by scanning

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:43.480
training data patterns.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.040
It is like a mirror that shows what the AI world sees when it looks at your name or interests.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:55.000
Curious teens and anyone who wonders how visible they are online will enjoy playing with it.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.400
Visit the site mentioned in the TechCrunch article and type in your name or a hobby to see

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.060
what comes up.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:07.860
The tool functions as an AI-centric vanity search that reveals patterns from training data about names

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:08.580
or topics.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:15.200
Now, for a couple of quick bits, a big study found that classes heavy on writing and coding saw

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.860
grades jump after chatGPT arrived.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.080
The increase happened mostly because students are using it to finish homework rather than learning

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:23.760
more deeply.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.880
The source reporting on the study is the-key-doter.com.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.280
The effect points to outsourced work instead of better learning overall.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.480
This raises honest questions about what grades really measure now.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:43.340
Classes that rely on writing and coding assignments showed the clearest change once the tool became

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:43.940
available.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:51.280
Open AI also made a deal so chatGPT can pull in actual images from Getty's library when answering

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:51.920
questions.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:55.680
The source reporting on the agreement is Engadget.com.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.080
You will get properly credited photos instead of AI-generated ones in many cases.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:07.340
The arrangement allows chatGPT to display real photographs from the Getty collection directly

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.080
in results.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.260
Users receive images that include proper attribution rather than generated alternatives.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:20.580
The partnership focuses on integrating the existing library into responses for relevant queries.

